A||||4||MotherGoose||Fuel Filter Question||||1||
Z||000000||MotherGoose||06-08-2000||01:25 PM||||If I installed the fuel filter backwards would it run a while then quit?  Would it restrict the fuel flow to a dribble?<P>Should the flow arrow be pointing the passager side or driver side?  I have a GT.<P>Thanks for all the help guys.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||06-08-2000||01:31 PM||||If I recall correctly the fuel line runs from the tank and turns toward the passenger side through the filter and then up to the engine.  So the arrow should face toward the passenger side.<P>I'm not sure if the fuel flows from the inside of the filter outward or vice-versa.  If it does and it is in backwards I guess the paper element could collapse on itself and blind of the flow.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MotherGoose||06-08-2000||02:03 PM||||I looked in my "Chilton" manual, and it says "make sure the flow is correct", and the picture is a drawing.  So I can't tell.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||06-08-2000||03:32 PM||||MotherGoose, I just changed the fuel filter on my '86 GT.  Doesn't it suck?!  It took me an hour, I had about an inch clearance above my face to work my hands.  Would've really sucked if I had gotten gas in my face.  Also burned my arm on the 500 degree catalytic convertor.  That really hurt!  To answer your question, yes, the arrow has to point toward the passenger side.  The old one I had pointed the arrow toward the passenger side, and that was on for 60,000+ miles w/o any problems.||||reg||10
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-08-2000||05:39 PM||||110,xxx miles and counting.  Getting replaced next week.  My mechanic got smart and squirted some penetrating oil on it last time I was in the shop.  Hopefully it'll come loose fairly easily.||||reg||1
